        intclMonIdeal(I,t)
  * Inputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the leading monomials of the
        elements of the ideal are considered as generators of a monomial ideal
      *  the free variable of the ring that should be used for the computation
        (optional), a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * allComputations (missing documentation) => ..., default value false, 
      * grading (missing documentation) => ..., default value {}, 
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the integral closure of the input
        ideal
      * a *note monomial subalgebra: MonomialSubalgebra,, the normalization of
        the Rees algebra of I

Description
===========


The leading monomials of the elements of I are considered as generators of a
monomial ideal. This function computes the integral closure of I\subset R in
the polynomial ring R[t] and the normalization of its Rees algebra. If
f_1,\ldots,f_m are the monomial generators of I, then the normalization of the
Rees algebra is the integral closure of K[f_1t,\ldots,f_nt] in R[t]. For a
definition of the Rees algebra (or Rees ring) see Bruns and Herzog,
Cohen-Macaulay rings, Cambridge University Press 1998, p. 182. The function
returns the integral closure of the ideal I and the normalization of its Rees
algebra. If there is a free variable in the original ring (i.e. a variable that
does not appear in any of the generators of I), you can give the function that
variable as second input. The function then uses it instead of creating a new
