nmzDataPath -- global variable, the path where Normaliz stores its files
************************************************************************

Description
===========


This global variable stores the file path where Normaliz stores the files
written. By default it is the empty string which means that the files are
stored in the current directory. If no filename is specified, this variable is
not used. There is no check whether the assigned directory exists. If it does
not exist, Normaliz will issue an error message. Use e.g. *note makeDirectory:
(Macaulay2Doc)makeDirectory_lpString_rp, to create an directory within Macaulay
2.
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : nmzDataPath                                 |
|                                                 |
|o1 =                                             |
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : nmzDataPath="d:/Normaliz2.5Windows/example";|
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : nmzDataPath                                 |
|                                                 |
|o3 = d:/Normaliz2.5Windows/example               |
+-------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Note that the path should not contain $ since Macaulay 2 seems to have problems
with such paths.

See also
========

  * *note Keeping results of the computation by Normaliz: Keeping results of
    the computation by Normaliz,

For the programmer
==================

The object *note nmzDataPath: nmzDataPath, is a *note string:
(Macaulay2Doc)String,.

